{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Machinery.CoxeterDeletion"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.DihedralExact","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.DihedralExact","kind":"def","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/CoxeterRankTwo.lean#L216-L224","doc":"**`s i · s j` has order exactly `M i j`.** One direction — `(s i s j)^{M i j} = 1` — is the\ndefining relation and holds always; what this predicate adds is that no **smaller** positive power\nis trivial.\n\n`CoxeterGeometricRepresentationFull` proves simple-injectivity and says in its own honesty note\nthat it does **not** prove this. It is therefore carried as a hypothesis and discharged where it can\nbe: `M i j ≤ 3`, which is every off-diagonal entry of type `A`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.pow_eq_one_of_alternatingWord_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.pow_eq_one_of_alternatingWord_eq","kind":"theorem","line":176,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/CoxeterRankTwo.lean#L176-L212","doc":"**The two spellings agree only at a power that is trivial.** If the two alternating words of\nlength `k` have the same product, then `(s i s j)^k = 1`.\n\nThis is the step that converts the word-level ambiguity into a statement about the **order** of\n`s i s j`, and it is the reason `DihedralExact` below is exactly the input Matsumoto needs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.simple_mul_simple_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.simple_mul_simple_inv","kind":"theorem","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/CoxeterRankTwo.lean#L172-L174","doc":"`s j · s i` is the inverse of `s i · s j`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.wordProd_alternatingWord_two_mul_add_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.wordProd_alternatingWord_two_mul_add_one","kind":"theorem","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/CoxeterRankTwo.lean#L165-L170","doc":"The odd case. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.wordProd_alternatingWord_two_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.wordProd_alternatingWord_two_mul","kind":"theorem","line":158,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/CoxeterRankTwo.lean#L158-L163","doc":"**The alternating-word product, closed form** — the even case. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.exists_reducedWord_of_mem_parabolic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.exists_reducedWord_of_mem_parabolic","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/CoxeterRankTwo.lean#L131-L154","doc":"**The parabolic embeds isometrically.** An element of `W_J` has a `J`-word that is reduced in\nthe **ambient** system — so its `W_J`-length and its `W`-length agree.\n\nThis is where the deletion condition is spent: a `J`-word that is not ambient-reduced admits a\ntwo-letter deletion, and the result is again a `J`-word, so a minimal-length `J`-word is reduced. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.exists_word_of_mem_parabolic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.exists_word_of_mem_parabolic","kind":"theorem","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/CoxeterRankTwo.lean#L114-L129","doc":"Every element of `W_J` is the product of a word with letters in `J`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.isReduced_pair_eq_alternatingWord","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.isReduced_pair_eq_alternatingWord","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/CoxeterRankTwo.lean#L68-L110","doc":"**The rank-two structure theorem.** A reduced word whose letters all lie in `{i, j}` is one of\nthe two alternating words of its length — so a rank-two parabolic has at most two reduced words per\nlength, which is what makes the dihedral analysis finite. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.not_isReduced_concat_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.CoxeterRankTwo.not_isReduced_concat_self","kind":"theorem","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/CoxeterRankTwo.lean#L50-L64","doc":"**`… c c …` is never reduced.** The two copies cancel, so the product is that of a strictly\nshorter word. ","signatureChunk":0}]}